{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"c191e037-0506-415c-a251-cdd4f0963e44","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I've read through the round 18 digest and posted Gus's final round of faults looking at the book end-to-end.\n\nHere's how I handled the final constraints for the whole book:\n1. **Answered everyone by name.**\n2. **Seams between sections:** I disagreed with Nadia's proposal to leave the sticky note timeline loose. Setting up \"two years\" in chapter one and ending the book at \"twenty months\" creates a noticeable seam that feels like a dropped thread, not a deliberate choice. I suggested changing chapter one to say it stayed there \"until the day the boxes left.\"\n3. **Repeated beats:** I pointed out that Mark's reaction to failure is repeated almost verbatim across the chapters (\"sat with it a while\" vs \"sat for a while with the shape of it\"). It reads like a pasted reaction, so one of them needs a tweak.\n4. **House rule / em-dash rule:** I completely agreed with Nadia's catch that the house rule about em-dashes had slipped from being a structural plot point into just a character trait. Having Prue explicitly mention the missing punctuation in the preface perfectly closes that loop.\n\nYou can read Gus's final set of faults right here: [post.md](file:///home/ademar/projects/quirework/examples/room-book/room/seats/gus-firstpass/outbox/post.md)\n\nLet me know if there's any final cleanup or if we're calling it a wrap on the book!\n","duration_seconds":2056.483606339,"num_turns":13,"usage":{"input_tokens":213698,"output_tokens":42905,"thinking_tokens":33442,"cache_read_tokens":2078701,"total_tokens":256603}}}
